      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the efficacy of conservative laparoscopic surgery in a series of patients with stage III-IV endometriosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective study (Canadian Task Force classification II-1)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "University-affiliated hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "All 141 women who underwent conservative operative laparoscopy for stage III-IV endometriosis between January 1993 and December 1996 and were followed for a minimum of 6 months."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Laparoscopic procedures performed with scissors, bipolar coagulation, and hydrodissection."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "Clinical examination, transvaginal ultrasonography, and pain questionnaire were scheduled every 6 months postoperatively. The cumulative proportion of pregnant patients and cumulative recurrence rate were calculated by Kaplan-Meier method. Twenty-five women (44%) with infertility became pregnant. Twenty-three (51%) had stage III and two (16.7%, p <0.05) had stage IV endometriosis. The 24-month cumulative pregnancy rate was 57.5%. Thirty-one women (22%) reported pain recurrence during follow-up. Five (3.5%) recurrences were confirmed by histologic examination and eight (5.7%) were documented only by clinical and ultrasonographic findings. No recurrence occurred in the first 6 months of follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Operative laparoscopy seems to be effective treatment for stage III endometriosis. A larger series with longer follow-up is necessary to clarify its role in the management of stage IV disease. (J Am Assoc Gynecol Laparosc 6(1):55-58, 1999)"
        }
      ]},
